# Command Injection (CMI) Attack Taxonomy

Command Injection (CMI) occurs when an application passes unsafe user-supplied data to a system shell. In this scenario, the operating system executes the user-supplied data as system commands with the privileges of the vulnerable application.

This taxonomy documents the primary special characters and control sequences used in command injection payloads, their shell contexts (Linux `bash`/`sh` vs. Windows `cmd.exe`), and how they are interpreted. This is a critical reference for evaluating and training WAF models, as real-world HTTP traffic contains payloads targeting both backends.

---

## Command Injection Control Characters

| Character | Shell Context | Effect | Minimal Example |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `;` | `bash/sh` | **Command Separator:** Executes the second command immediately after the first completes, regardless of the exit status of the first command. (Has no separator effect in `cmd.exe`). | `cat file.txt; id` |
| `\|` | `both` | **Pipe / Redirection:** Redirects the standard output (stdout) of the left-hand command to the standard input (stdin) of the right-hand command. Often used to chain arbitrary utilities. | `echo hello \| id` |
| `&&` | `both` | **Conditional AND:** Executes the second command only if the first command completes successfully (exits with code `0`). | `ping -c 1 8.8.8.8 && id` *(Linux)*<br>`ping -n 1 8.8.8.8 && whoami` *(Windows)* |
| `\|\|` | `both` | **Conditional OR:** Executes the second command only if the first command fails (exits with a non-zero code). | `false \|\| id` *(Linux)*<br>`dir non_existent_file \|\| whoami` *(Windows)* |
| `` ` `` (backtick) | `bash/sh` | **Command Substitution:** Executes the command inside the backticks in a subshell and replaces the backtick expression with the command's standard output. (Does not work in `cmd.exe`). | `` echo `id` `` |
| `$()` | `bash/sh` | **Command Substitution (Modern):** Executes the command inside the parentheses in a subshell and replaces the expression with the stdout. Supports nesting. (Does not work in `cmd.exe`). | `echo $(id)` |
| `&` | `both` | **Asynchronous / Command Separator:**<br>• `bash/sh`: Backgrounds the first command (runs asynchronously) and runs the second command immediately.<br>• `cmd.exe`: Sequential separator. Operates identically to `;` in Linux, running the second command after the first completes. | `sleep 10 & id` *(Linux)*<br>`dir & whoami` *(Windows)* |
| `\n` (newline) | `both` | **Line / Command Separator:** Breaks commands into separate lines. In CMI, it acts as a separator when the application processes inputs line-by-line or executes multi-line command strings directly in the shell. | `id\nwhoami` *(Linux)*<br>`dir\nwhoami` *(Windows)* |
| `%0a` | `both` | **URL-encoded Line Feed:** When the web application URL-decodes user inputs prior to shell execution, `%0a` becomes a raw newline (`\n`), acting as a stealthy command separator that bypasses basic character blocklists. | `id%0awhoami` |
| `%00` | `both` | **URL-encoded Null Byte:** String terminator in C/C++ and many backend languages (e.g., PHP, Perl, Python). Used in CMI to truncate trailing characters or file extensions appended by the application logic, ensuring the injected command is executed cleanly. | `id; %00` *(prevents trailing appended args from running)*<br>`cat /etc/passwd%00.txt` *(bypasses trailing extension checks)* |

